speechtext:
Subsection 2 refers to Acts that may be passed, but subsection 3 refers to Acts heretofore passed. Under the old law subsection 3 was a section by itself, but it went further than the first subsection of this section 3 does, because it says ;
This Act, subject to any express provisions of the special Act and to the exception hereinafter mentioned, applies to all persons, companies and railways, within the legislative authority of the parliament of Canada, except government railways.
So, if you strike out subsection 2, you would change the law as it heretofore existed. I am not sure of this point, but I am submitting it for the consideration of the committee. The old law provided, in case there might be any doubt, that the special Act was specially authorized to change the General Railway Act. Now, whether that was necessary or not is a question for us to consider. But, we found it necessary in 18S8, when we consolidated the Act, to make special provisions, that the special Act overrode the Railway Act. I do not know whether it is the intention of the minister, but it might affect my judgment to learn, whether it was his intention to divide that section into three subdivisions ?
